message:
Fix a bug where ordered host callbacks with no return values were relying on sends blocking until callback completion
When a host callback has no return values but has an ordered side effect, `emit_python_callback` uses the send's completion as a token to serialize the next host callback per the ordered side effect. This happens to work today because TPUs are blocking the send completion until the callback returns, but is brittle because there's no guarantee that the TPU runtime should do so. In fact, the fact that the send's token doesn't become ready until the host callback runs to completion is a bug in the TPU runtime as this is currently achieved by blocking the driver thread, which (unintentionally) blocks other unrelated operations.
This CL fixes this bug by adding a dummy return value to the callback in such a case. With this change, `emit_python_callback` takes a token from the recv instead of a send and now relies on the fact that the recv doesn't complete until the host callback returns, which has to be true regardless of the thread on which the host callback is run.
HLO dump from `IOCallbackTest.test_sequence_pjit_io_callback_ordered` before/after this CL:
